perf/park-not-spin — BOUNDED SPIN THEN YIELD, MEASURED — 2026-08-09
====================================================================

Host      Apple M4 Pro, 8 P-cores + 4 E-cores (12 logical), macOS 26.5.2,
          rustc 1.97.1. THE CORE COUNT IS THE RESULT: t=8 fits the P-cores,
          t=16 oversubscribes. No -C target-cpu=native. No `nice` on any
          timed run (Darwin maps nice to E-cores, ~40x distortion).
Arms      base = main @ ee07b00, head = perf/park-not-spin @ 407c156.
          Two staged binaries, `bench_ab_decode`, one config per process.
Method    scripts/perf/verify_gap.sh, two-point wall fit (2 and 20 frames,
          total = alpha + beta*frames, so process startup drops out),
          arms interleaved with a ROTATING order inside every cell, STRICT
          idle gate. Every committed row has foreign_max = 0; the box was
          shared with other agents and the gate discarded and re-ran the
          affected cells rather than admitting them.
n         16 rounds on v4k_8tile at every thread count; 14-16 on
          v4k_8tile_10b (the box went busy indefinitely before the 10bpc
          t=8/t=16 cells reached 16 — stated rather than padded).
Records   park_not_spin_gap_2026-08-09.tsv (310 rows)
          park_not_spin_bands_2026-08-09.txt (per-arm bands + ratios)
          park_not_spin_lockstats_2026-08-09.tsv (contention probe, TAGGED
          LOADED — see below)
Harness   scripts/perf/tail_report.py (new), scripts/perf/tail_sweep.sh (new,
          per-decode sampler; NOT used for the headline, see "not measured"),
          --features probe-lockstats (new, crates/rav1d-disjoint-mut).


HEADLINE — THE PREMISE IS WRONG AND THE CHANGE IS STILL WORTH HAVING
---------------------------------------------------------------------

The branch was written on the theory that an unbounded spinner produces a fat
tail with a normal median, and asked to be judged on p90/max and band width.
Measured on an idle box at n=16, that theory does not hold and a different,
smaller, real effect does:

| vec           | t  | n  | base med | head med | r_med | r_p90 | r_max | band base | band head |
|---------------|----|----|----------|----------|-------|-------|-------|-----------|-----------|
| v4k_8tile     | 1  | 16 |   318.39 |   319.69 | 1.004 | 1.000 | 0.998 |  25.50    |  19.00    |
| v4k_8tile     | 2  | 16 |   198.25 |   198.42 | 1.001 | 1.005 | 0.999 |   3.61    |   3.00    |
| v4k_8tile     | 4  | 16 |   107.39 |   106.97 | 0.996 | 0.994 | 0.979 |   5.44    |   2.78    |
| v4k_8tile     | 8  | 16 |    67.44 |    67.86 | 1.006 | 0.998 | 1.003 |   5.17    |   5.94    |
| **v4k_8tile** |**16**|16| **72.97**| **69.69**|**0.955**|**0.944**|**0.944**| 6.06 |   4.94    |
| v4k_8tile_10b | 1  | 16 |   359.94 |   360.00 | 1.000 | 1.001 | 1.000 |   4.06    |   4.28    |
| v4k_8tile_10b | 2  | 16 |   206.61 |   206.53 | 1.000 | 0.999 | 1.000 |   4.89    |   4.89    |
| v4k_8tile_10b | 4  | 15 |   110.89 |   112.00 | 1.010 | 0.989 | 1.006 |   6.06    |   5.78    |
| v4k_8tile_10b | 8  | 14 |    71.19 |    72.58 | 1.020 | 1.018 | 1.055 |   4.56    |   8.78    |
|**v4k_8tile_10b**|**16**|14| **77.19**| **74.14**|**0.960**|**0.969**|**0.971**| 4.39 |   6.50    |

1. **The win exists and it is at t=16 only: 0.955 (8bpc) and 0.960 (10bpc)
   on the MEDIAN.** Bands at 8bpc t=16 are base [69.22..75.28] against head
   [66.11..71.06] — they touch over 1.8 ms of a 6 ms span, and p90 and max
   move by the same 0.944 as the median, i.e. the whole distribution shifted
   rather than its tail being clipped.

2. **t=1, 2, 4 and 8 are null on every statistic, both depths.** t=16 is the
   only cell on this box where threads exceed cores, and it is the only cell
   that moves. That is the mechanism stated as a measurement rather than an
   argument.

3. **THERE IS NO FAT TAIL TO COLLAPSE.** Base band widths are 7.7% of the
   median at 8bpc t=8 and 6.4% at 10bpc t=8 — not the ~60% the branch's
   rationale assumed. Head's bands are not systematically narrower; at 10bpc
   t=8 head's band is nearly twice as wide (12.1% vs 6.4%) and at 8bpc t=8 it
   is wider too (8.8% vs 7.7%). **Judged on the criterion the PR asked for —
   p90, max, band width at t=4/8 — this change measures null.** It survives
   on a different result than the one it predicted.

4. **The band evidence the PR cites is not reproducible and has no source.**
   The quoted 8bpc t=8 bands [67.8..114.6] and [73.2..112.8] appear in no
   committed benchmark TSV in this repo (I recomputed per-cell bands over
   every gap-shaped TSV in benchmarks/) and in no issue or PR body except
   #471 itself. The fattest committed t=8 bands are in
   scaling_gap_dav1d_2026-08-08.tsv — `plain` [72.33..97.89] (33%) — and that
   file is one its own meta already tags as run with ALLOW_LOAD=1 under a
   foreign qemu process in 36 of 60 rows. In the SAME file, **dav1d, which
   contains none of our locking, has bands of [34.28..57.78] (64%) and
   [32.11..43.06] (32%)**. Those fat t=8 bands are a property of a loaded
   box, not of TinyLock.


MECHANISM — probe-lockstats, and a correction to the campaign's numbers
------------------------------------------------------------------------

New throwaway probe `--features probe-lockstats`
(`rav1d_disjoint_mut::lock_probe`): one `fetch_add` per CONTENDED acquisition
(none on the fast path, none inside the spin loop — iterations accumulate in a
local). It counts `TinyLock::lock_slow` entries, total `spin_loop()`
iterations, `yield_now()` calls and the longest single acquisition.
Perturbation check: this build runs v4k_8tile 8bpc t=8 at 71.9 ms/frame
against 67.4 un-probed, so it is a usable counter — unlike `probe-count`,
which runs the same cell at 6,537 ms/frame and whose own 19.7% contention
figure is entirely self-inflicted.

**The "~0.02% contention" in the PR body is the wrong counter.** The 23,009
figure is `wide_probe::N_SLOW`, which counts `BorrowTracker::add_slow` — the
poisoned / live-wide-record / multi-block path — and has nothing to do with
lock contention. Measured directly (v4k_8tile 8bpc, 6 frames, t=8):

  registrations/frame      22,700,725   (probe-count on base, measured here)
  lock acquisitions/frame  45,401,450   (add + remove)
  contended acquisitions   150,858 - 198,316 per 6-frame run
                           => ~27,800/frame => 0.061% of acquisitions,
                              0.122% of registrations — SIX TIMES the
                              0.02% quoted.

One `spin_loop()` costs **8.28 ns net** on this host (`isb`, not a nop;
20 M-iteration loop, best of 5, minus an identical empty loop). That prices
the counters:

| arm  | t  | contended | spin iters | yields | max spins in one acquisition |
|------|----|-----------|-----------|--------|------------------------------|
| base | 4  |  191,445  | 1,688,666 |      0 | 1,083  (~9.0 us)             |
| base | 8  |  150,858  | 1,322,714 |      0 | 1,387  (~11.5 us)            |
| base | 16 |  192,249  | 2,033,586 |      0 | **13,262 (~110 us)**         |
| head | 4  |  167,968  | 1,297,033 |  3,044 |   900                        |
| head | 8  |  198,316  | 1,528,031 |  2,657 |   841                        |
| head | 16 |  270,246  | 2,175,310 |  3,638 | 1,179                        |

* **Liveness: the 64-spin bound IS reached** — 2,657-3,638 yields per 6-frame
  run, i.e. ~1.4% of contended acquisitions. The change is not inert. 98.6%
  of contended acquisitions still resolve inside 64 spins, which is why t<=8
  cannot move.
* **The unbounded spin is real but small.** The worst single acquisition
  burned ~110 us at t=16 and ~11.5 us at t=8. That is two orders of magnitude
  short of a preemption quantum, and it is why the predicted 45 ms tail was
  never going to be there.
* Aggregate spin work at t=16 falls ~23% (2.21 M -> 1.74 M iterations per
  6 frames, interleaved n=5 medians) = 0.64 ms/frame of CPU, against a
  3.3 ms/frame WALL win. **Most of the t=16 gain is therefore the holder
  getting a core back, not the waiter's own burn being avoided.**
* head's `max spins` at t=16 occasionally exceeds base's (7,616 and 14,848 in
  the loaded series). This is a counter artifact, not a regression: the head
  counter accumulates across yields, so a long head wait is 100+ yields with
  at most 64 contiguous spins between them, whereas every base spin is
  contiguous burn. Contiguous burn is bounded at 64 by construction on head.

park_not_spin_lockstats_2026-08-09.tsv (n=5 interleaved rounds) is **TAGGED
LOADED** — a foreign 100%-CPU process held one core throughout. Its counts and
its base/head ratios are usable (0.949 at 8bpc t=16, matching the idle sweep's
0.955); its absolute ms are not.


CORRECTNESS
-----------
* Corpus **766/766**, `md5_inventory` set-diff BY NAME against
  benchmarks/aarch64_md5_fixes_2026-08-07_final.tsv.zst with the actual md5 as
  the value: only-in-baseline 0, only-in-head 0, **differing 0**.
* Output byte-identical base vs head, 10/10 cells (v4k_8tile and
  v4k_8tile_10b at t=1/2/4/8/16): a00c11f454328023c58af14d55544cff and
  4f218411bc6ee4cc9c630fe827337fa2 on both arms at every thread count.
* `cargo test --release -p rav1d-disjoint-mut` green (26 lib + 25 soundness +
  5 shard_liveness + narrow_release + 1 rowmap); `wide_exclusion` green under
  `--features __probe_wide`.
* `mt_stress` 1/2/4/8/16 x5 PASS.
* `scripts/perf/multi_decoder_pressure.sh` 12 procs x 3 iters x threads
  1/2/4/8/16 PASS, every md5 matching the serial reference.
* Instrument verified against the binary, not the source: `otool -tvV` on
  `TinyLock::lock_slow` shows base as a frameless leaf looping on
  `isb`/`ldrb` with no exit, and head with `cmp w8,#0x3e` + `bl _sched_yield`.
  head's `lock_slow` gains an `stp x29,x30` frame; `lock`/`try_lock`/`unlock`
  are unchanged single atomics in both, so the frameless leaf `add` is intact.


NOT MEASURED — do not extrapolate
----------------------------------
* **x86_64.** Nothing here transfers: `spin_loop()` is `pause` there with a
  completely different cost, and the scheduler is not heterogeneous.
* **The oversubscription ladder.** t=12 (== logical cores), t=24 and t=32 were
  queued and never got an idle window — two other agents held the box. Whether
  the t=16 win grows, saturates or reverses past 16 is UNKNOWN, and the
  "more threads than cores" mechanism is inferred from a single oversubscribed
  cell plus the lock counters, not from a ladder.
* **Any vector other than v4k_8tile / v4k_8tile_10b.** In particular nothing
  with loop restoration live, which is the campaign's standing blind spot.
* **A per-decode distribution.** scripts/perf/tail_sweep.sh was written to
  sample every decode individually (the right instrument for a rare event,
  since 16 two-point rounds is only 16 tail samples) and never ran, for the
  same box-contention reason. The tail conclusion above therefore rests on 16
  rounds per cell, which is enough to say the base band is ~8% and not ~60%,
  and NOT enough to characterise a 1-in-1000 event.
* **`--features unchecked`**, Miri, and any spin bound other than 64. The 64
  was not swept; it is the value the deleted probe used.
